    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_90de62bedb2dcf1cbb27d83bfa2ca4900ca59f686be8d86fe86d418a6f4ba19f",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "For a compactly supported good kernel already lying in the L2 closure of B(P f), V=I-2K_2 preserves the assumed compact support while enforcing zero mean. That cancellation gives P(Vf) and its generated approximants a common tail cutoff, making Mellin evaluation at a fixed zeta zero an L2-continuous bounded-interval functional. Muntz's formula kills the approximants there, so the limit forces f-hat to vanish at that zero; hence closure yields Z(zeta) contained in Z(f-hat), and a separately assumed zero-free f-hat upgrades the result to the RH equivalence.",
